# MindMitra — Caregiver Location Tracking

Adds a feature to MindMitra AI: a person can turn on live location sharing, and any
caregiver they've registered (via a pairing code) can see where they are on a map,
in real time — until the person turns it off.

## How it fits together

```
frontend/patient-app/index.html      ← the original MindMitra app, with location
                                        sharing added (Settings + Caregiver mode)
frontend/caregiver-dashboard/        ← a small separate web app for caregivers,
                                        showing a live map (Leaflet + OpenStreetMap)
backend/                             ← Node/Express + Socket.IO API that both
                                        of the above talk to
```

**Processing in between:** the patient app pushes GPS readings to the backend over
REST (`POST /api/location/ping`); the backend stores the latest point and also
broadcasts it over a Socket.IO room (`patient:<id>`) so any connected caregiver
dashboard updates instantly, without polling.

### Consent model
- Only the **patient's own token** can turn sharing on/off (`POST /api/patients/sharing`).
  A caregiver can never enable tracking themselves.
- A caregiver is only linked after the patient hands them a **6-digit pairing code**
  shown in Settings. Nobody can add themselves without that code.
- The patient can **revoke** any caregiver's access at any time from Settings — that
  caregiver immediately loses the ability to read location data (server-side check,
  not just hidden in the UI).
- Turning sharing off stops new pings from being accepted or broadcast — it doesn't
  just hide the map on the caregiver's screen.

## Running it locally

### 1. Backend
```bash
cd backend
npm install
cp .env.example .env
npm start        # or: npm run dev  (with nodemon, auto-restart)
```
This starts the API + Socket.IO server at `http://localhost:4000`. It stores data in
`backend/data/db.json` (created automatically on first run — a plain JSON file, fine
for development, see "Going to production" below).

### 2. Patient app
Open `frontend/patient-app/index.html` directly in a browser (or serve it with any
static server, e.g. `npx serve frontend/patient-app`). It talks to the backend at
`http://localhost:4000` by default. To point it at a different backend URL, set this
before the app's script runs:
```html
<script>window.MINDMITRA_API_BASE = 'https://your-backend.example.com';</script>
```
In the app: go to **Settings → Location sharing**, turn it on (the browser will ask
for location permission), and copy the pairing code shown there.

### 3. Caregiver dashboard
Open `frontend/caregiver-dashboard/index.html` in a browser (a different device,
tab, or browser profile works well for testing). Same `window.MINDMITRA_API_BASE`
override applies here too. Enter the pairing code from step 2 to link, and the live
map appears once the patient has a GPS fix.

## API summary

| Method | Path | Who calls it | What it does |
|---|---|---|---|
| POST | `/api/patients/register` | patient app, once | Creates the patient record, returns `token` + `pairingCode` |
| GET | `/api/patients/me` | patient app | Own profile, sharing state, linked caregivers |
| POST | `/api/patients/sharing` | patient app | Turns sharing on/off |
| POST | `/api/patients/pairing-code/rotate` | patient app | Issues a fresh pairing code |
| POST | `/api/caregivers/link` | caregiver dashboard | Links a caregiver using a pairing code |
| DELETE | `/api/caregivers/:id` | patient app | Revokes one caregiver's access |
| GET | `/api/caregivers/me` | caregiver dashboard | The caregiver's own linked-patient info |
| POST | `/api/location/ping` | patient app | Pushes a new GPS reading (rejected if sharing is off) |
| GET | `/api/location/current` | caregiver dashboard | Latest known point |
| GET | `/api/location/history` | caregiver dashboard | Last ~50 points, for a trail on the map |

All patient/caregiver routes expect `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.

## Going to production

This is a working starter, not a hardened deployment. Before using it with real
patients, at minimum:
- Replace the JSON file store (`backend/src/db.js`) with a real database.
- Replace token-only auth with proper authentication (passwords/OTP + short-lived
  tokens, refresh tokens, rate limiting on `/api/caregivers/link` so pairing codes
  can't be brute-forced).
- Serve everything over HTTPS/WSS only.
- Add audit logging of who viewed a patient's location and when.
- Review India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act (DPDP) requirements for
  health-adjacent location data, since this handles sensitive personal information
  about a potentially vulnerable user group.